 { Shoulder-chargingAs we have seen, the rook needs support from the king to control and capture the enemy pawn. That is why the defending king should always stand in the way to the pawn. Shouldering is always an interesting resource, but it becomes critical when the stronger side's king is approaching from the side. Many times, in order to help the rook, the king has to stand in front of his opponent and then the enemy king is pushed one rank (or file) back by means of a rook check. The purpose of this manoeuvre is twofold: 1 To bring our king closer. 2 To restrict the enemy king's (and/ or pawn's) mobility. We will name this way of struggling for a key zone 'shoulder-charging' or just 'pushing'. Of course, all winning chances are for the rook, but only in the long run. Sometimes there is no time to exploit the advantage. } 1. Kg4 $1 { Visually the best move, though not the only way to win. The white king tries to reach the g3 -square on the next move and then push the enemy king. The threat is 2. Re1+. If you have realised that } ( 1. Kh4 $1 { is the other winning move, it means you have grasped the essence of the kings' struggle. The point is that, if White succeeds in establishing a king opposition on e3 and g3 - } Ke3 2. Kg3 { - as in the game, Black will not escape from the checks. } ) 
Ke3 ( 1... d3 $6 { allows White to push the king: } 2. Re1 Kd4 3. Kf3 d2 4. Rd1 Kd3 5. Kf2 $18 ) 
2. Kg3 $1 { Again, using opposition to push the black king, who cannot escape because he must stay near his pawn. The immediate check does not work } ( 2. Re1 $6 Kf2 $5 { White must go back to the right path } 3. Rd1 $1 Ke3 4. Kg3 ) 
d3 3. Re1+ Kd2 4. Kf2 $18 { And the white king has made it to the key zone and can aid his rook. } 
