 { Outflanking. The importance of zugzwangIn contrast with pawn endings, or positions with less powerful pieces, zugzwang positions very rarely appear with a rook on the board. Why ? Because a rook usually has many moves at its disposal, and it is unlikely that they should all be harmful. Anyway, zugzwang plays a decisive role in a Rook vs. Pawn ending when the rook controls the pawn from a frontal position. Réti, 1928 This famous Réti composition illustrates a zugzwang situation that holds theoretical value. } 1. Re2 $1 ( { or } 1. Re3 { . } ) 
 ( { For the time being, we are more interested in analysing the natural move } 1. Re1 $2 { and then } e4 $1 { Here is the zugzwang position we want to discuss. King opposition prevents the white king from approaching the pawn; any rook move on the first rank allows the pawn to move forward; a rook move to e2 or e3, or a check on f1, will only allow Black to gain time. } 2. Ke7 { is the most logical attempt: the white king tries to outflank his opponent } ( 2. Rf1 Kg4 $1 3. Ke6 e3 4. Ke5 e2 { (the aforementioned gain of time) } 5. Ra1 Kf3 6. Kd4 Kf2 $11 ) 
Ke5 $1 { Maintaining the king opposition is essential. This is another zugzwang position: White's outflanking succeeds against any other move: } ( 2. Re2 Kf4 $2 3. Ke6 e3 4. Kd5 Kf3 5. Kd4 e2 6. Kd3 { and the king has succeeded. } ) 
3. Kd7 Kd5 $11 { Keeping the opposition and the zugzwang. There is no way to make progress. } ) 
e4 2. Re1 $1 { If you have paid attention to all the previous comments and lines, you will easily understand the rest. Black cannot keep the opposition. It is a mutual zugzwang. } Ke5 3. Ke7 $1 { Now the black king has to allow outflanking. } Kf4 ( { Or } 3... Kd4 4. Kf6 { etc. } ) 
4. Kd6 Kf3 5. Kd5 e3 6. Kd4 $18 
